---
import { brand } from '../data/brand';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
---
<Base title="How it works" description={`How buying, downloading and printing ${brand.name} files works, with material and print setting advice.`}>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <Breadcrumbs items={[['Home', '/'], ['How it works']]} />
      <h1>How it works</h1>
      <p class="lede">Two ways to shop: accessories we ship to you, and 3D files you download and print.</p>
      <h2 id="accessories">Accessories</h2>
      <p>Physical products ship to your address. Shipping cost and estimated delivery are shown at checkout before you pay. Every listing says if it is universal or which cars it fits.</p>
      <h2 id="files">3D print files</h2>
      <p>You buy a digital file, not a physical part. Here is what you get and how to turn it into a part on your car.</p>
      <h2>What's in every download</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>STL</strong> files for any slicer.</li>
        <li>Fitment, years, material and print settings on every product page.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2 id="printing">Printing guide</h2>
      <p><strong>PETG</strong> for cabin parts out of direct sun: clips, bushings, knobs. <strong>ASA</strong> for anything exposed to UV or heat: exterior parts, dash tops, engine bay. Avoid PLA for car parts; it softens in a parked car.</p>
      <p>Use at least 4 walls on clips and anything that takes load. Print bushings at 100% infill.</p>
      <h2 id="no-printer">No printer?</h2>
      <p>Send the STL to a local print shop, a makerspace, or an online print service. Ask for the material listed on the tech sheet.</p>
      <h2>Licenses</h2>
      <p>Our files are based on designs published under licenses that allow commercial use (CC BY, CC BY-SA, CC0), always credited. See <a href="/legal/attributions/">licenses & attributions</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
